@identity.sigil from Identity import { session gate, actor }
@rooms.sigil from Rooms import { room, room owner, room timezone, room lock }
@availability.sigil from Availability import { open time, clock, slot grid }
@booking.sigil from Booking import {
  booking request,
  Pending,
  Confirmed,
  no-overlap rule,
  availability workflow,
  archive workflow,
  timezone workflow,
}
@calendar.sigil from Calendar import { room calendar }

component Slotted {
  goal {
    Slotted is a reference *room-booking app* in which any signed-in user can
    list a room and request time on other users' rooms, with owner approval and
    a room calendar.

    The room-booking app exists to exercise Sigil design and a full build
    pipeline on a domain with two roles, a booking request lifecycle, time-zone
    arithmetic and a concurrency invariant.
  }

  interface {
    Identity owns sign-up, sign-in and the session gate that resolves each
    request to an actor.

    Rooms owns each room, its owner, its timezone, archiving, and the
    room lock that serializes changes to one room.

    Availability owns weekly windows, blackouts and open time, and defines
    the meaning of the slot grid and the clock.

    Booking owns every booking request, its lifecycle, the no-overlap rule,
    and the availability workflow, archive workflow and timezone workflow.

    Calendar owns the room calendar, a read-only projection of open time and
    booking request state for one viewer.

    The *app layer* is the set of pages and Server Actions; it offers every
    screen of the room-booking app and calls modules only through their
    *public entry*.
  }

  state {
    Each module is one *module boundary* with a public entry, private
    internals, and, for Rooms and Availability, a *transaction entry* that
    exposes the mutations only a Booking workflow may invoke: archive, unarchive
    and room timezone change for Rooms, and weekly window and blackout changes
    for Availability.

    The *shared kernel* holds the clock, slot grid arithmetic, time-zone and
    daylight saving conversion, database access and domain errors, and is used
    by every module boundary.
  }

  logic {
    A request reaches the app layer, which passes the session gate to obtain
    the actor.

    A Server Action in the app layer then calls one command or workflow on a
    public entry, and a page calls the queries it renders, each passing the
    actor and the clock.

    A command that changes one room runs inside that room's room lock, and a
    change that crosses modules runs as one Booking workflow in that lock.

    The app layer then refreshes the page so the room calendar and lists show
    the new booking request state.
  }

  constraints {
    architecture {
      The room-booking app must be a modular monolith shipped as one Next.js
      application.

      The app layer must import each module boundary only through its
      public entry and must never import a transaction entry.

      Only Booking may import a transaction entry, so the availability workflow,
      archive workflow and timezone workflow are the only paths to those
      mutations.

      Each module boundary may depend only on these others, with no cycle:
      Booking on Availability and Rooms, Availability on Rooms, Rooms on
      Identity, and Calendar on Booking, Availability and Rooms.

      Lint rules must enforce the module boundary rules, and a dependency graph
      check must reject cycles and cross-module internal imports in CI.

      Mutations must be Server Actions and reads must be Server Components; the
      only Route Handler is the authentication handler that backs the
      session gate.

      Every Server Action except sign-up and sign-in must pass the session gate
      and check ownership itself, because a Server Action is a public endpoint.

      Booking request reads and the room calendar must never be cached.
    }

    technology {
      The app layer runs on Next.js 16.3.7 or later, required before the first
      merge, with the App Router, React 19,
      TypeScript 6.0 and Node 22, with cache components turned off.

      The sign-in technology behind the session gate is decided by Identity.

      Persistence for every module boundary uses PostgreSQL 18 and Drizzle ORM
      0.45 with generated and migrated schemas and never schema push, over one
      pg driver shared with the session gate.

      The no-overlap rule on Confirmed booking request records is enforced by a
      PostgreSQL exclusion constraint written as a hand-written SQL migration.

      Time arithmetic in the shared kernel uses the Temporal API through the
      temporal-polyfill package, and the clock is the only source of now.

      The module boundary rules are enforced with eslint-plugin-boundaries and
      dependency-cruiser.

      Tests for the room-booking app use Vitest 5 for unit tests and for
      integration tests against a real PostgreSQL 18 database, because mocks
      cannot show the no-overlap rule or the room lock, and Playwright 1.63 for
      end-to-end tests against a production build.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    architecture {
      A modular monolith keeps the room-booking app in one deployable while the
      module boundary rules keep ownership as clear as separate services would.

      Booking owns every cross-module workflow because each room change needs a
      sweep or check over booking request records inside one room lock.

      Passing the actor and the clock explicitly keeps the session gate and time
      testable outside a request.

      The database backs the no-overlap rule, not only application checks, so
      concurrent approvals can never both confirm a booking request.
    }

    technology {
      Drizzle was chosen over Prisma for the persistence of every
      module boundary because the Prisma release tagged latest is an 8.0
      release candidate that the sign-in technology chosen by Identity does not
      support, and range columns need hand-written SQL either way.

      The app layer stays on TypeScript 6.0 because typescript-eslint does not
      yet support 7, and the shared kernel keeps the Temporal polyfill until the
      runtime moves to Node 26.
    }
  }

  cases {
    A page imports a Rooms transaction entry directly; lint fails and CI
    rejects the change because only Booking may import it.

    The room owner approves two overlapping booking request records from two
    tabs at once; the room lock serializes them, exactly one becomes Confirmed
    and the other is no longer Pending, with the exclusion constraint as the
    backstop.

    A booking request is approved; the Server Action refreshes the page and
    the room calendar shows the Confirmed block.
  }
}
